  "constraintReading": "With up to 1000 numbers per list, merging is fast in practice, but the problem asks for O(log(m + n)), which rules out reading every number. Binary search on how many items the shorter list puts in the left half takes O(log(min(m, n))) steps, within O(log(m + n)), the O(log n) choice on the Plan card. Only a few indexes are stored: O(1) space.",
  "hints": {
    "clarify": "What is the median when the count is even? The average of the two middle numbers, so the answer can be a fraction: [1, 4] and [2, 6] merge to [1, 2, 4, 6], and the median is (2 + 4) / 2 = 3.0. Can one list be empty? Yes, then it's the median of the other.",
    "approach": "Binary search on a cut. Picture the merged list split into a left half and a right half, with the left half one item bigger when the count is odd. The left half is the first i items of the shorter list `a` plus the first j items of `b`, with i + j fixed, so binary search i from 0 to len(a). A cut is right when `a[i − 1]` ≤ `b[j]` and `b[j − 1]` ≤ `a[i]`. If `a[i − 1]` is too big, a gave too many items: move i left. If `b[j − 1]` is too big, a gave too few: move i right. At the right cut, the median comes from the largest item on the left and, for an even count, the smallest on the right.",
    "whyNot": "Merging the lists and reading the middle is O(m + n). Walking two pointers to the middle without building the merged list saves the space but still reads half the numbers. Binary searching the value of the median instead of the cut needs extra counting with care for repeats; searching the cut in the shorter list is simpler and touches only a few numbers.",
    "slots": {
      "setup": "Make `a` the shorter list. `half` = (m + n + 1) // 2 items go to the left half. Search i, the number taken from `a`, between `lo` = 0 and `hi` = len(a).",
      "loop": "Keep going while `lo` ≤ `hi`; a right cut always exists, so the loop returns from inside.",
      "update": "With j = half − i: if `a[i − 1]` > `b[j]`, set `hi` = i − 1; if `b[j − 1]` > `a[i]`, set `lo` = i + 1. Treat a missing neighbor (i or j at an end) as no obstacle.",
      "record": "Once neither test fires, the cut is right: the left half's largest item is the bigger of `a[i − 1]` and `b[j − 1]`, the right half's smallest is the smaller of `a[i]` and `b[j]`.",
      "return": "For an odd total, return the left half's largest item. For an even total, return the average of it and the right half's smallest item."
    }
  },
  "solution": {
    "code": "class Solution:\n    def findMedianSortedArrays(self, nums1: list[int], nums2: list[int]) -> float:\n        a, b = (nums1, nums2) if len(nums1) <= len(nums2) else (nums2, nums1)\n        total = len(a) + len(b)\n        half = (total + 1) // 2  # how many items the left half holds\n        lo, hi = 0, len(a)\n        while lo <= hi:\n            i = (lo + hi) // 2  # items a gives to the left half\n            j = half - i  # items b gives\n            if i > 0 and j < len(b) and a[i - 1] > b[j]:  # viz:cut\n                hi = i - 1  # viz:too_many\n            elif j > 0 and i < len(a) and b[j - 1] > a[i]:\n                lo = i + 1  # viz:too_few\n            else:\n                left = max(a[i - 1] if i > 0 else b[j - 1], b[j - 1] if j > 0 else a[i - 1])  # viz:found\n                if total % 2 == 1:\n                    return float(left)  # viz:odd\n                right = min(a[i] if i < len(a) else b[j], b[j] if j < len(b) else a[i])\n                return (left + right) / 2  # viz:even\n        return 0.0\n",
    "explanation": "The search runs on the shorter list, so it takes about log₂(min(m, n)) + 1 steps: O(log(m + n)) time at most, the O(log n) choice on the Plan card. Only indexes and two values are stored: O(1) space. A right cut always exists, so the final `return 0.0` never runs. Searching the shorter list also keeps j = half − i between 0 and len(b) for every i tried.",
    "toolkit": []
  },
